# Bring-Up and Testing Plan

Use a current-limited bench supply (start at 100 mA limit) and **do not connect a fan or the MCU programmer until steps 1–4 pass**.

| Step | Check | Expected result |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Visual inspection, continuity from the DC jack to GND with power off | No short between `VCC` and GND |
| 2 | Apply 20 V, measure `VCC` at `C6` | ≈ 20 V, low idle current |
| 3 | Measure 12 V rail (`C4`) | ≈ 12 V (±3%) |
| 4 | Measure 5 V rail (`C23`) and 3.3 V rail (`C8`) | ≈ 4.95 V and ≈ 3.3 V |
| 5 | Red LED `D3` | Lights when 3.3 V is present |
| 6 | Connect ST-LINK to `J3` and read device ID | STM32F411 detected |
| 7 | Read `PA4` voltage at room temperature | ≈ 0.75 V at 25 °C (TMP36) |
| 8 | Warm the sensor by hand | Voltage rises by about 10 mV per °C |
| 9 | Drive `PA6` high, no fan connected | Drain of `Q1` pulled to ≈ 0 V |
| 10 | Connect fan, test 0 %, 50 %, 100 % duty | Fan speed follows duty; `D4` clamps the turn-off spike |
| 11 | Thermal check at full fan load | Buck ICs, inductors, `D4` and `Q1` within safe temperature; sensor reading not skewed by board heating |
| 12 | Test across 20 V, 22.5 V and 25 V | Rails remain in regulation |

Record the measured values in a table (date, board serial, rail voltages, idle current, fan current) and commit it to the repository under `docs/`.

# Design Overview

## 1. Requirements

| # | Requirement | Implementation |
|---|---|---|
| R1 | Operate from a single 20–25 V DC supply | DC jack `J1` feeding two buck converters |
| R2 | Drive a 12 V DC fan | 12 V buck (`U2`), low-side MOSFET `Q1`, fan terminal `J5` |
| R3 | Measure temperature | TMP36 (`U5`) read by MCU ADC on `PA4` |
| R4 | Control fan speed automatically | PWM on `PA6` from the STM32F411 |
| R5 | In-circuit programming and debug | SWD header `J3` |

## 2. Power Tree

```text
J1 (20–25 V)
 ├── U2 TPS563300  ──► 12 V ──► Fan (J5)
 └── U1 TPS54202   ──► 5 V  ──► U3 AMS1117-3.3 ──► 3.3 V ──► MCU, TMP36, LEDs
```

### 12 V rail (U2)

- Inductor `L1` 12 µH; output capacitor `C4` 47 µF
- Feedback divider `R1` = 140 kΩ (top), `R3` = 10 kΩ (bottom); ratio 15:1, implying a 0.8 V feedback reference for 12 V out
- Input capacitors `C6` 10 µF / 50 V and `C7` 100 nF; bootstrap `C2` 100 nF

### 5 V rail (U1)

- Inductor `L2` 15 µH; output capacitor `C23` 47 µF; feed-forward `C3` 68 pF
- Feedback divider `R2` = 100 kΩ, `R4` = 13.7 kΩ:
  `Vout = 0.596 V × (1 + 100 / 13.7) ≈ 4.95 V` (typical reference; confirm in the datasheet)
- Input capacitor `C5` 6.8 µF / 35 V; bootstrap `C1` 100 nF

### 3.3 V rail (U3)

- AMS1117-3.3 fed from the 5 V rail; `C9` (input) and `C8` (output) 10 µF
- Dissipation at full load: `(5 V − 3.3 V) × I`. At 100 mA this is about 0.17 W, which is comfortable for SOT-223.

## 3. Sensing Chain

```text
3.3 V ─► TMP36 (U5) ── Vout ── R13 1 kΩ ──┬── PA4 (ADC)
          │ C21 0.1 µF bypass            └── C22 100 nF ─ GND
          SHUTDOWN tied to 3.3 V
```

`T(°C) = (Vout − 0.5 V) / 0.010 V/°C`. The RC filter has a corner frequency of about 1.6 kHz (`1 / (2π · 1 kΩ · 100 nF)`), which removes ADC-sampling kickback and high-frequency noise while remaining fast compared to thermal dynamics.

## 4. Fan Driver

- `Q1` AO3400A (30 V, 5.8 A, logic-level) switches the fan's return path to ground
- `R14` 150 Ω limits gate-charge current from `PA6`
- `R15` 10 kΩ holds the gate low while the MCU pin is high-impedance (reset/boot), keeping the fan off
- `D4` SS14 clamps inductive flyback when the fan is turned off

## 5. Microcontroller Support Circuitry

| Function | Parts |
|---|---|
| Reset | `S2` push button, `R6` 10 kΩ pull-up, `C17` 0.1 µF |
| Supply decoupling | `C12`–`C14` 0.1 µF on 3.3 V |
| Analog reference | `L3` ferrite bead (150 Ω @ 100 MHz), `C15`/`C16` 0.1 µF on `VREF+` |
| Core regulator | `C18` 1 µF on `VCAP_1` |
| 32.768 kHz clock | `Y2` active oscillator, `C20` 8 pF, `R12` 10 kΩ (tri-state pull-up), output to `PC14` |
| Debug | `J3` 4-pin SWD: SWDIO (`PA13`), SWCLK (`PA14`), 3.3 V, GND |
| Indicators | `D2` blue LED + `R9` 1.5 kΩ on `PC13`; `D3` red LED + `R10` 5.1 kΩ (power) |

## 6. Mechanical / Layout Notes

- Irregular board outline, with the DC jack on the upper left, the fan terminal at the lower left and the SWD header at the lower right
- Power stages (inductors, diode, buck ICs) are grouped on the left half; the MCU and its decoupling are on the lower right
- Schematic sheets: Main System (A4), Power (A4), MCU (A3), Sensor (A4), Actuator (A4)

# Design Review Notes

Items found while reviewing the schematic, BOM and PCB layout. Severity: **High** = verify before fabrication, **Med** = recommended, **Low** = polish.

| # | Sev. | Observation | Recommendation |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | High | `C18` (1 µF) is on `VCAP_1` of the STM32F411 | Confirm the value required by the STM32F411 datasheet for `VCAP_1` and correct it if it is not 1 µF |
| 2 | High | `BOOT0` is not visibly tied to a defined level on the MCU sheet | Add a pull-down (for example 10 kΩ) to GND, or a jumper, so the MCU boots from Flash reliably |
| 3 | High | No reverse-polarity or surge protection on the 20–25 V input | Add a series Schottky/P-MOSFET and a TVS diode at `J1` |
| 4 | Med | `C4` and `C23` are 47 µF / 16 V X5R; DC-bias derating reduces ceramic capacitance a lot. `C8`/`C9` (10 µF / 25 V) are already noted in the BOM as derating to about 3.5 µF | Use higher-voltage-rating or larger-case parts, and check regulator stability and output ripple against the datasheets |
| 5 | Med | Fan current is limited by `D4` (SS14, 1 A) and the 12 V rail (3 A) | State the maximum supported fan current (≤ 1 A) in the documentation, or upsize `D4` |
| 6 | Med | `U5` (TMP36) sits close to the 12 V inductor/diode area, so self-heating can bias the reading | Move the sensor away from power components, or onto a thermally isolated spur |
| 7 | Med | LED currents are very low: blue `D2` (Vf ≈ 3.2 V) through 1.5 kΩ from 3.3 V gets ≈ 0.07 mA; red `D3` through 5.1 kΩ gets ≈ 0.28 mA | Lower `R9`/`R10` (for example 330 Ω for red; use the 5 V rail or a lower-Vf LED for blue) |
| 8 | Low | BOM descriptions for `C18`, `C20`, `R4` and several 0402 resistors are copied from other parts, and manufacturer part numbers are empty | Fill in real manufacturer part numbers and supplier data before ordering |
| 9 | Low | Schematic title block (title, revision, author) is empty | Fill in title, revision and author on every sheet |
| 10 | Low | No fan tachometer input or over-temperature indicator | Consider for a future revision (3-wire fan support) |
